{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation","id":1452694,"title":"The XP Disconnect and advanced character creation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-17T14:43:43-07:00","id":1452694,"title":"The XP Disconnect and advanced character creation","content":"Hello everyone.<br /> <br /> So I've always felt there has been somewhat of a disconnect between the amount of experience points (xp) being awarded basically 1 xp per night survived and the starting xp the books suggest when creating an older/advanced character than the default. Although, the books does state either at the end of each game session or chapter, and while it is definitely possible for a session to encompass more than one night, I think for the majority of sessions, it would be one session equals one night.<br /> <br /> The minimum amount of xp a character can be awarded for surviving the night, hence if a kindred were to be up every night in a full year, it would be getting 365 xp - which ties into the xp disconnect I'm getting to.<br /> <br /> According to the sidebar on p.79 in the V20 core book, for instance, it suggests between 0-35 xp for a neonate, I understand this as it basically marks the 0 value as the point of entry for neonates, where above 0 would be an older and more advanced character being created. However, it doesn't make any note about how much time it represents the kindred being active, nor if the amount of xp represents a value to be factorized in relation with age (in years). But as stated, if a kindred of whatever age can be awarded up to 365 xp for a full year of being active and surviving the nights, how does it make sense that a character should receive and xp amount that is significantly lower but represents a much longer time interval?<br /> <br /> The sidebar also states that as a basic rule of thumb, &quot;idle&quot; kindred should have a number of discipline dots equal to the square root of it's age. So basically, a neonate character at 100 years of age, should have at least 10 dots in disciplines, it starts with 3, so 7 to go, at the bare minimum, this is 10x7 = 70 xp. Player characters are active characters, so they would rapidly outpace this, which to my understanding translates into - they should actually be starting with way more xp than that. Besides this, the experience points it would've cost to increase to these levels, could've been spent on other character aspects instead, to reflect character progression in other areas than disciplines.<br /> <br /> Age Category and their approximate age intervals (in years), and xp intervals:<br /> Fledgling: 0-25| 0<br /> Neonate: 25-100 | 0-35<br /> Ancillae: 100-250 | 75-220<br /> Elder:250-750| 250<br /> Methuselah: 750-1000<br /> Antidiluvian: 1000+<br /> <br /> However, using age categories (status titles) to reflect age, is a bit ambigious, because it doesn't actually reflect age, what it really reflects is social kindred maturity level within the established kindred society, and not actual age. So creating an older and more advanced, using a status title to determine starting xp as opposed to it's actual amount of activity (for instance measured in years) is a bit odd and difficult to work with. Personally, I view age as something that the ST sets the certain amount of xp that is suitable for the chronicle, I think the elder background could be suitable as a reference point, but not something the players should actually spend points on acquiring.<br /> <br /> I think, having adjusted templates for the age categories is probably a much better way of handling it, and I wonder if something like this already exists for V:tM?<br /> <br /> With regards to the XP disconnect, what are your thoughts?<br /> <br /> //Inc.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24617-incarnate","user":"Incarnate","date":". 06-17-2021, 08:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24617-incarnate","name":"Incarnate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T15:27:13-07:00","id":1452702,"title":"","content":"I think there are multiple levels of disconnect inherent in what the system is trying to do.<br /> <br /> On the one hand, people want to play the vampires from their favorite movies/novels, and the system is designed to allow them to do that in a fairly reasonable amount of time. For example, making Jerry Dandrige from &quot;Fright Night&quot; takes about 210 experience. That's a lot of xp, but you can at least get close in a long campaign.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, they want their NPCs to fit into general categories of Poor/Fair/Good/Excellent/Amazing, and if you gave them XP at the rate they get it while playing, any character over five years old at the longest would be in the Amazing category.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T15:27:43-07:00","id":1452703,"title":"","content":"It's true, because V20 actually awards xp per session, and in theory up to 5, with possible bonus xp at the end of each story. Even if you assume (correctly) that the average Neonate is not going out there and testing their abilities each night as player characters do, you're still going to end up very powerful before long. Originally, the game awarded you 1-3 xp at the end of each STORY, not Chapter, and that's probably more in line with those guidelines, as a Story could represent a large amount of time in-game. Personally, I like somewhere in the middle, usually 10 xp at the end fo the Story, with occasional awards during chapters. But after a year of play, this can make for very strong characters. This isn't unjustifiable, however, there are plenty of established NPC's in the setting who are much stronger than their age would suggest. What I tell my players is, if they ever look at someone's stats and say &quot;hey he's not that tough&quot;, that most vampires, especially most Elders, are sandbagging and hiding their true power. What you see in a stat block is simply the most powerful Traits they have been seen using to date. Ultimately the decision of how much xp to give to your players is a personal decision that you have to decide upon- do you like the characters being slow to develop, or prefer them to quickly move into the sphere of Ancilla, regardless of their true age?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T16:16:56-07:00","id":1452708,"title":"","content":"To my mind, where this breaks down is in the assumption that every character is doing 1 XP's worth of growth every night, forever. Downtime happens, and Vampires are notoriously stagnant creatures whose breaks from significant activity can last years, decades, or centuries at a time.<br /> <br /> What seems like a low amount of free XP for a character who starts off very old can easily represent a lot of time that got lost to humdrum responsibilities, torpor, or getting stuck in any other kind of rut.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T16:32:13-07:00","id":1452712,"title":"","content":"I think the &quot;1xp per night&quot; position is disputable. The one game that I've run had weeks of time passing by during a single session, with players getting 1-3xp per session generally. The point made above about activity is really relevant, because how active you are during a given time period, and the nature of that activity, could have a significant impact on the experience points that you get. A character shouldn't get an experience point for a night they just spent sitting on the couch, slurping from a blood-doll while they watch the news. In my opinion, they shouldn't get an experience point even if they are very active for the night, but the activity is routine or normal for them. Experience points should be the results of circumstances that cause characters to grow, not the simple passage of time.<br /> <br /> Also, I think it's important to consider that those experience totals, regardless of what Age or rank they are supposed to represent, are meant for starting characters. Even within those various categories, there will be ranges in abilities and resources. The experience totals provided in that table might be suggested for the weakest or lowest-ranking characters that would nonetheless qualify for the rank/title in question. And having your new characters start at the low end of the average rank and ability for their age makes sense, actually; you want your players to have something to be hungry for (other than blood). Obviously a Storyteller can let their characters start with any amount of XP to begin with, but those numbers might make more sense if you see them as being made for characters that are still supposed to have a lot of growing to do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T16:47:46-07:00","id":1452715,"title":"","content":"My excuse is that all vampires experiment a growth spurt/teenage period in wich their powers devolop quickly until they reach their potential/adulhood and then their increase in power slows down or just stops unless they are actively pursuing power wich most don\u00b4t explaining power diferences between vampires.<br /> <br /> The players characters are simply taking way more risks during this period wich results in overgrowth as their beast survival instinct is overloaded making them evolve above their peers.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T17:12:08-07:00","id":1452717,"title":"","content":"Just some food for thought, the big issue with character generation and age versus expectation from my perspective as a player and as a storyteller, is there typically seems to be a <i>massive</i> disconnect between how published NPC's are statted, versus how players prioritize character growth and experience expenditure. Published NPC's almost seem to get dots in attributes and abilities for free, whereas players will (predictably) prioritize disciplines for experience expenditure, and dip in attributes and abilities only as needed. I do it, my players do it, players in other groups I've communicated with do it, and other ST's I've communicated with do it.<br /> <br /> There's nothing inherently wrong with it, it just yields inconsistent returns looking at NPC stat blocks versus PC stat blocks. And, most elder NPC's will almost always be better-rounded characters than equivalently-aged PC's; they may not necessarily be <i>stronger</i>, because players are going to optimize their characters, they're just better-rounded and more organic-feeling.<br /> <br /> I'm taking a couple measures in my TC game to counteract it, and so far the results have been damn promising.<br /> <br /> First, I've increased maturation point awards from what's suggested in the DAV books, to three per decade with a fourth for proper blue booking. The caveat being, only half their maturation points can be spent on disciplines. Experience points awarded during the course of play can, of course, be spent however they please. So, that ensures they're building attributes, abilities, and virtues at a reasonable pace without really compromising discipline growth.<br /> <br /> The second measure is, I've created a standardized formula for NPC generation. Discipline dots equal to square root of age, and apply the experience points it would have taken to buy those dots to attributes, abilities, virtues, and derived stats. It's worked out pretty well so far, as I've been able to create NPC's with it that pretty closely approximate published characters. I recreated Lucita using my formula with both Dark Ages and modern nights ages, and her stats were for the most part an exact match for what's published.<br /> <br /> I'm still gathering data on it and doing regression analysis, but I'm coming closer to an actual mathematical formula that's a decent fit for approximating experience points as a factor of age.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T04:54:16-07:00","id":1452754,"title":"","content":"The difference in progression between PC and NPC is mostly a theoretical problem to me. Yes it could/does happen, but I have not experienced adverse effects.<br /> <br /> I alway found the experience gain by the books rules to be incredibly stingy. I run a story and people could buy perhaps one little thing. You than have to pay extra for backgrounds, rituals, loresheets, etc.<br /> <br /> I have always handed out more to have some sense of progression.<br /> <br /> This of course depends a lot on how long your session is in game (usually several days, sometimes weeks or just a day), how often you play (biweekly), how long you stories are (always more session than I thought/about 6).<br /> <br /> For my personal taste and preference I find it sad that I have to throw out the suggested version in the book immediately.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26030,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","user":"mfalkenb","date":". 06-18-2021, 08:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","name":"mfalkenb","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T05:53:06-07:00","id":1452764,"title":"","content":"One issue is that, for most vampires, their unlives are stable: The prince rules well, elder machinations are subtle, politics rarely interferes with them so long as they stay in their own lane, nobody is hunting them, they remain within their comfort zone for feeding, and they can easily delegate to a retainer for the regular small issues. They arent under much pressure to improve, while PCs are almost always thrown to the messiest domains where they are constantly challenged.<br /> <br /> Second issue is that players are given xp to do what they will with, while NPCs gain skills naturally, and attributes/disciplines are presumably cultivated through diet/method of hunting. Letting players use a 'natural' method would be complicated and upset the balance of the game, as the Nosferatu feeding from be\u0123gars is likely going to be at a disadvantage compared to the Toreador and their pick of Ballerinos and professors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 06-18-2021, 11:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T13:19:44-07:00","id":1452814,"title":"","content":"An option you could use is to grant more XP, but split it among Disciplines/Attributes/Abilities/miscellanea (virtues, humanity, backgrounds, rituals which wouldn't be free if you buy them this way) <br /> So, say, your players get 8 xp in a session, but they can only spend 2 on Disciplines, 2 on attributes, 2 on abilities and 2 on misc <br /> This isn't overly complicated, but get your vampires to develop more &quot;naturally&quot;, as much as that word can apply to an undead predator that grows through parasitism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T14:42:50-07:00","id":1452823,"title":"","content":"Well one thing you can do is enforce the old rule that you can only increase each trait by one dot per story. That really forces players to make well-rounded characters. Depending on how long each story within your campaign lasts, it can easily mean that no one trait increases by more than one every couple of months, even if you hand out XP like water.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T18:07:27-07:00","id":1452838,"title":"","content":"Another fix is to divide EXP into two types: Mundane and Sanguine. More total EXP can be given out without destroying character balance, since over a period of sessions a PC might pick up 20 exp to put into their Attributes/Abilities and 10 exp to put into their Disciplines, while 30 exp they could spend on anything would have likely all gone to Disciplines.<br /> <br /> This helps simulate a more broad progression of a player character - an aging Toreador doesn't just master Celerity, they master the viola and the spoken barb and the ability to keep up with what's in vogue. A player who loves to develop dubiously useful but highly flavourful abilities isn't punished by being compared to PCs with insane Disciplines, and a player who loves the mechanical side of things gets to enjoy investing in abilities that they otherwise would feel compelled to ignore by the shiny blood magic paths.<br /> <br /> I first tested this in MTA and it worked very well, with Mundane and Arcane EXP respectively covering things like Attributes and Spheres.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T18:21:06-07:00","id":1452840,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation?p=1452838#post1452838\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I first tested this in MTA and it worked very well, with Mundane and Arcane EXP respectively covering things like Attributes and Spheres.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do something similar in my MtAs games, but I require players have the total experience value to buy the dot in question up front, then refund experience for the purpose of buying attributes and abilities, based on how well they roleplayed their Seeking or the process of developing a new Sphere rating.<br /> <br /> I've toyed with the idea of doing something similar with disciplines, but couldn't <i>quite</i> make it work in my head for most situations. There's ample room for roleplaying the first time a character earth melds, practices dread gaze, or does something like shove a dumpster down an alleyway to test how stronger that new dot of Potence makes them, but it's not something <i>easily</i> universalized -- not to mention how these scenes may tend to have a lighter tone which clashes with the ongoing story or chronicle -- so I shelved the idea. Disciplines are notably cheaper than Arete or spheres, so the overall cost-effectiveness of such a measure is certainly a concern as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T00:57:17-07:00","id":1452870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation?p=1452840#post1452840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I do something similar in my MtAs games, but I require players have the total experience value to buy the dot in question up front, then refund experience for the purpose of buying attributes and abilities, based on how well they roleplayed their Seeking or the process of developing a new Sphere rating.<br /> <br /> I've toyed with the idea of doing something similar with disciplines, but couldn't <i>quite</i> make it work in my head for most situations. There's ample room for roleplaying the first time a character earth melds, practices dread gaze, or does something like shove a dumpster down an alleyway to test how stronger that new dot of Potence makes them, but it's not something <i>easily</i> universalized -- not to mention how these scenes may tend to have a lighter tone which clashes with the ongoing story or chronicle -- so I shelved the idea. Disciplines are notably cheaper than Arete or spheres, so the overall cost-effectiveness of such a measure is certainly a concern as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I tend to give 2 normal experience points for every supernatural experience point. and then give slightly more experience in total than I would have normally. This means Disciplines progress more slowly than they would in a normal game, but the Vampire progresses in a more rounded way. That said I imagine it could mean that certain high level Discipline powers take longer to unlock, but that's not necessarily a bad thing given that mastering a Discipline is meant to take a long time, and the highest levels of a power should be weighed up against the opportunity to buy many smaller things. For example, 20 EXP on Obfuscate 5, or should I buy Celerity 1 and Dominate 1 as I go?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T01:47:56-07:00","id":1452873,"title":"","content":"I'm surprised no one seems to like the rule that already exists: each trait can only be raised one dot per story. That rule, which has existed forever, means that even if you dish out 200 xp in the course of one story, each discipline can only be raised one dot. So if you begin with, let's say, Protean 1, Dominate 1, Potence 1, then the most you will have by the end of the story is Protean 2, Dominate 2, Potence 2, and 1 in all other Disciplines, and that's true even if you get 200, 300, even 400 xp. You literally have to spend the points to get well-rounded, because there's nothing else to do with your pile of XP.<br /> <br /> By RAW, a character who received a literal billion XP in one story would, at most, have plus-one to each Attribute, Ability, Discipline, and Humanity/Path, while all the rest of the XP sat there unspendable until the next story.<br /> <br /> I think maybe it never caught on because the end of stories isn't always clearly defined. Many games can be rather soap-opera-y, a series of subplots that get don't get resolved until new subplots are already in play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3598,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","user":"CajunKhan","date":". 06-19-2021, 04:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T04:06:14-07:00","id":1452880,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation?p=1452873#post1452873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm surprised no one seems to like the rule that already exists: each trait can only be raised one dot per story. That rule, which has existed forever, means that even if you dish out 200 xp in the course of one story, each discipline can only be raised one dot. So if you begin with, let's say, Protean 1, Dominate 1, Potence 1, then the most you will have by the end of the story is Protean 2, Dominate 2, Potence 2, and 1 in all other Disciplines, and that's true even if you get 200, 300, even 400 xp. You literally have to spend the points to get well-rounded, because there's nothing else to do with your pile of XP.<br /> <br /> By RAW, a character who received a literal billion XP in one story would, at most, have plus-one to each Attribute, Ability, Discipline, and Humanity/Path, while all the rest of the XP sat there unspendable until the next story.<br /> <br /> I think maybe it never caught on because the end of stories isn't always clearly defined. Many games can be rather soap-opera-y, a series of subplots that get don't get resolved until new subplots are already in play.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a very, very good point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T06:59:39-07:00","id":1452892,"title":"","content":"&quot;Story&quot; isn't a helpful term given that it could be two one hour sessions or it could be twenty ten hour sessions (19 of which are spent on one combat encounter) <br /> I kinda want my games to be untidy too. I can just give them a nice, neat self-contained story, but ideally my players should be throwing fuel onto whatever fire I've got in store for them. <br /> <br /> You should also consider that players might still hoard XP they can't use. We play a &quot;story&quot; and let's say I buy a dot of Potence and Obfuscate. I want to buy more Potence and obfuscate, but the story hasn't ended, so I keep my XP. The story ends, I use my stockpile to buy more Potence and Obfuscate, and mostly wait for the next story. In the beggining, it seems I'm just hoarding XP that I could put to good use, but as Potence and Obfuscate get more expensive, the XP surplus becomes necessary as Obfuscate is now costing more than what I could get in a single story. <br /> <br /> A lot of XP is also spent on disciplines because, well, you can really be rocking out the gate when it comes to abilities and attributes, but disciplines aren't that cost efficient at character creation. 2 freebies to push your 3 dots skill into a 4 is generally a better investment than doing the same with disciplines. You can start the game as a &quot;master&quot; of all the skills you want to use, but you can't really do that so much with disciplines, and if you do throw all your freebies into disciplines it generally feels like you're a janky powergamer who doesn't care for story, which might not work at some tables. Lastly, with abilities and attributes your target is generally 4 dots, which grants you a speciality; each dot grants you a proportionately lesser boost and as 5 dots doesn't offer a speciality, it is extremely cost inefficient. However, buying the 5th dot of a discipline usually grants you something more than just another die, so it makes sense to keep investing in them. <br /> <br /> That seems a little obtuse, so let me give an example. <br /> I want to play a nosferatu, an agressive but otherwise fairly typical member of the clan. I prioritize the Mentals, Strength, Manipulation... I can get 4 4's there without freebies. The abilities I'll want high are Alertness, stealth, larceny, brawl, Intimidation and subterfuge. I can spend 12 freebies to have 4's in all these abilities, though I probably won't do that for all of them. After picking some flaws I spend most of the rest of my freebies on willpower; I am now -good- at everything I need to &quot;succeed&quot; in the game. Making my best skills better is a poor use of XP. I can either improve some skills I'm bad at and improve my range, or I can spend all my XP on disciplines; because Potence 1 is a much better investment than strength 5. <br /> <br /> From an RP standpoint it also makes sense to have more normal skills than vampire stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-20T01:17:03-07:00","id":1452933,"title":"","content":"I stopped giving out exp in my White Wolf games years and years ago. Instead I went with a dot system, so it would be like, &quot;Okay everyone, at the end of the session everyone gets a dot they can put into an Attribute of your choice, as well as one dot into a non-favored Ability. Also Joe, I noticed you had some great roleplaying this session and your character has been actively working on studying a lot about ghosts, so he also gets an additional dot of Occult as well as a specialty involving Ghosts. At the end of the next session everyone will be getting an additional dot in a Clan Discipline so spend the next week thinking about what you might want to grab, but keep in mind you won't be able to increase any of your Disciplines above 4 just yet.&quot;<br /> <br /> I've found that by giving out dots, but sometimes relegating those dots to unfavored Abilities, or &quot;any Ability with less than 3 dots&quot; or &quot;increase your lowest Attribute by 1,&quot; etc, it tends to end up making characters feel more organic. It also lets me give out more rewards to players since it's a little bit harder to min-max. Finally it helps me to control the power-scale of the game a little bit more. My group's always generally liked it, since they end up getting more rewards than they would under the exp system, even if those rewards aren't always things that they would choose to spend exp on if we were using the actual exp system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 06-20-2021, 04:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-11T12:32:27-07:00","id":1454702,"title":"","content":"This is the system I use, I have been testing it for more than 5 years and it works pretty well.<br /> <br /> - Each session every PC gets 1 XP. Maybe 2 if the session has been very intense or formative.<br /> - In case of downtime, every PC gets 1 XP per year.<br /> <br /> We recently run a one-shot playing ancillae, 80 year old. We generated the PCs as usual and then gave them 80 XP. It worked pretty well. I have found is quite useful as a rough guideline for non-overpowered NPCs, and then you can adjust as needed.<br /> <br /> If we play as RAW the XP inflation is ridiculous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26110-ramon-nogueras","name":"Ramon Nogueras","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-11T14:18:15-07:00","id":1454708,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Incarnate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation#post1452694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello everyone.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think, having adjusted templates for the age categories is probably a much better way of handling it, and I wonder if something like this already exists for V:tM?<br /> <br /> With regards to the XP disconnect, what are your thoughts?<br /> <br /> //Inc.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it does in Elysium: The Elder wars. There is a base elder template as well as the Age background which gives more freebies (up to 100 at 5 dots for 750-1000 years active with -5 humanity. but it also increases EXP costs by quite a bit.) There is a lot more to it all in all with a lot of discussion and mechanics around elder characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/724-palp","name":"Palp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar724_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-11T23:59:42-07:00","id":1454722,"title":"","content":"Why not use the Magical Character Beans rules from the last chapter of Chronicles of Darkness: Mirrors? The name is intentionally silly but they suggest you can also call it destiny points or fate points or something like that. <br /> <br /> That system lets you set a PC's potential growth upfront like so:<br /> <br /> All players start with 30, 40, 50, 60 or 100 MCBs. These can be spent like Freebies. The ST may rule that you can only spend up to half of these (rounded down) at chargen. The rest are your future potential, which you will grow into over the course of a chronicle, but with a helping hand to get you there until the points are all spent.<br /> <br /> For each MCB left over after chargen, you get that many dice per story to affect your destiny. So you can add a die to a dice pool (up to +5 dice on any one roll), trade a die to buy and frame a scene of your choice, or permanently sacrifice a die to use as a Freebie Point and advance your character. I'd probably also add things like buying a clue or introducing a fact, depending on the expertise of the PCs.<br /> <br /> Any spent MCBs are replenished at the end of the story, except those spent on advancement (which are gone forever). You may also recover one or two for good roleplaying, as the ST deems fit. <br /> <br /> In addition, the character with the highest remaining MCBs counts as the 'main' protagonist for the purposes of getting the spotlight. Those with fewer MCBs have less room for growth, and so the story isn't really about them.<br /> <br /> This creates some nice incentives: you probably don't want to blow everything right away, because you lose those extra dice, the dramatic editing and the spotlight. But from time to time, you want to increase your traits too. Additionally, some players will be happy to spend everything early so they can play a powerful supporting role--the muscle or the mentor, for example. So long as they're okay with the game focussing on the characters with more scope for advancement, it's cool.<br /> <br /> It also fixes the XP issue totally. And you can use the same rules for NPCs. If you want more powerful NPCs, have them spend all their MCBs upfront. They're powerful, but have no dramatic editing, so the PCs still have a chance and can save up those extra dice for important rolls.<br /> <br /> I would probably also take a leaf from Warhammer's Fate Points and let a PC spend, say, five MCBs to completely avoid death. They get a miraculous Hollywood survival like Loomis after Halloween II or like any of those 'dead' soap opera characters that returns later and goes, 'Surprise! I didn't really die, after all!' It lets players take a bigger risk every now and again, but not all the time. You could limit it to one lucky escape per story (or even per chronicle!), or you could be really harsh and say it costs you all your remaining MCBs.<br /> <br /> For more open-ended games, you could perhaps add new MCBs to the total to extend the life of the characters and give them further room to grow. In that case, you might want to start lower at the start (e.g., with 30 MCBs), so that PCs are still only growing a small amount over the course of a story. Each new story, they gain another +15 MCBs or so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 07-12-2021, 03:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-12T08:17:24-07:00","id":1454743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1452694-the-xp-disconnect-and-advanced-character-creation?p=1452933#post1452933\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I stopped giving out exp in my White Wolf games years and years ago. Instead I went with a dot system, so it would be like, &quot;Okay everyone, at the end of the session everyone gets a dot they can put into an Attribute of your choice, as well as one dot into a non-favored Ability.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also do this. I've stopped bothering with XP and just give PCs increases to their traits on an ad hoc basis. <br /> <br /> It's worked out pretty great for my group. You can tailor advancement rates to your game much more conveniently than XP rates, since the GM always has a complete picture of what PC stats do and could look like. If a game doesn't last long, you can give out lots of stats increases per session; if a game spans multiple RL years, you can hand out only one stat increase per session, or go some sessions without awarding any stat increases.<br /> <br /> It's also satisfying as a player just to get the extra dot right there, no dicking around with XP as a middleman.<br /> <br /> It's basically like getting rid of XP in D&amp;D/PF and telling PCs to level up at GM-determined story points. I've had nothing but good experiences doing that too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}